Coming and Going: [Sculpting/Content/What] Performers alternating entering and
exiting the composition (not physically) – ie. Soundpainter signs whole group coming
and going three – the ensemble tries to maintain three performers playing at one time.
The tug of war that often ensues during the group decision making is the real intent
of “coming and going”. There will be times, depending on size of the ensemble, when
more than 3 performers will naturally enter ie. In a large ensemble the Soundpainter
signs whole group, coming and going 3, enter slowly – as an example of what often
happens, because no performer knows who is going to enter, 8 performers might enter
– the individual player knows the “coming and going” number is 3 and must quickly
decides on whether to keep performing, becoming one of the 3 - or the performer may
choose to drop out leaving others in to establish the “3”. This dynamic of entering
and exiting in order to arrive at the 3 performers is the idea of “coming and going”. In
this example when the “3” performers have been established then another one or more
may enter – the tug of war begins all over – politeness is not the key! A performer may
choose to come in and force another player out. The material being performed is open
to the players unless other wise signed ie Soundpainter signs whole group, coming
and going 3, with, pointillism, slowly enter – in this example only “pointillism” is
performed by the ensemble.
