Play: Function/Go/When
Immediate initiation of signed material. The performer must come in right at the end
of the gesture and come in hard–edged.
Play Can’t Play 2, 3, 4, etc: Play Can’t Play is a series of cells (entrances). The
Soundpainter first signs the Play Can’t Play gesture followed by the number of cells
(entrances) to be performed. Each cell is initiated with a Play gesture. The performer
upon first seeing the Play Can’t Play gesture, and number of cells, must quickly decide
which cell to perform in, and once chosen will only perform in that cell. When the
performer’s cell is initiated the performer comes in right at the Play gesture (hard
edged), performing a single idea, developing it until the Soundpainter initiates the
next cell. Each performer develops their idea independent of the other performers –
as if you’re the only one in the room performing – a player may not relate to another
player. When the next cell is initiated, the performer(s) playing immediately stop (hard
edged), and the next performer(s) enter (hard edged), each with one idea they develop
independently of all the other performers.
The Soundpainter initiates the cells in order, each performer is responsible for remembering which cell they are in, and perform the next time their cell comes around. The
performer may choose to perform the same material, modify it, or perform new material. Note; the performer develops his/her idea at a moderate rate – at a quicker
development than Point to Point or Scanning, but not a full blown improvisation as if
taking a solo. Note; if the Soundpainter makes a Memory of one of the cells, then the
performer may not perform the material again – only in the Memory.
Play Can’t Play is a chance gesture and a search gesture. The Soundpainter does
not know what he/she will get. The Soundpainter may choose to stop on a cell and
develop it, or, may use the rotation from cell to cell as the direction of the composition.
Note: If the Soundpainter chooses to develop one of the cells, spending quite some
time doing so, and would like to return to Play Can’t Play, it is recommended to resign the gesture. This makes it brand new, as if the previously signed Play Can’t Play
didn’t exist. Note: There is a chance none of the ensemble will perform in one of the
cells, yielding silence, if this happens the Soundpainter must quickly decide how to
use it - don’t be surprised, Play Can’t Play is like rolling the dice. There are many
levels and complexities to Play Can’t Play – they will be discussed in great detail in
Soundpainting Workbook 5.

